# 21. Explain to an Interviewer — 60-Second Script 🎤

> Logistic Regression is a supervised machine learning algorithm mainly used for classification. It first calculates a linear combination of the input features and then passes that value through the sigmoid function, which converts it into a probability between zero and one. For binary classification, the model typically applies a decision threshold to convert the probability into a class label. It is trained by minimizing binary cross-entropy loss, usually using optimization methods such as gradient descent. Logistic Regression is computationally efficient and interpretable, making it a strong baseline for classification problems, although it can struggle with highly nonlinear relationships. In practice, I evaluate it using metrics such as precision, recall, F1-score, ROC-AUC, and a confusion matrix rather than relying only on accuracy.

---

# 22. Top 3 Interview Questions 🎯

## Q1. Why is Logistic Regression used for classification even though it has "Regression" in its name?

### Answer:

> Logistic Regression models the relationship between input features and the log-odds of a class. After applying the sigmoid function, the output becomes a probability that can be converted into a classification decision.

---

## Q2. Why do we use Binary Cross-Entropy instead of MSE?

### Answer:

> Binary Cross-Entropy is designed for probabilistic binary classification and strongly penalizes confident incorrect predictions. It also provides an optimization objective that is well suited to Logistic Regression.

---

## Q3. Accuracy is 99%. Is the model necessarily good?

### Answer:

> No. In an imbalanced dataset, a model can achieve high accuracy by predicting only the majority class. For problems like fraud detection or disease detection, metrics such as precision, recall, F1-score, PR-AUC, and ROC-AUC can provide a more meaningful evaluation.

